431	The following fact shows that we can derive a weaker converse in the case when only one of~$A$ or~$B$ is projective.
432	
433	\begin{fact}\label{fact:almost-agreement}
434	Suppose $\{A^x_a\}$ and $\{B^x_a\}$ are two measurements such that $A^x_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} \approx_{\delta} I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^x_a$.
435	Suppose further that either~$A$ or~$B$ is a projective measurement (and the other is a POVM measurement).
436	Then $A^x_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} \consistency_{\delta^{1/2}} I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^x_a$.
437	\end{fact}
438	\begin{proof}
439	Our goal is to upper bound the expression
440	\begin{equation}\label{eq:anand-is-a-stable-genius-yet-again}
441	1 - \E_\bx \sum_a \bra{\psi} A^{\bx}_a \otimes B^{\bx}_a \ket{\psi}.
442	\end{equation}
443	We begin by rewriting the number~$1$.
444	Here we use the fact that because~$A$ is projective, $(A^x_a)^2 = A^x_a$,
445	and so $\sum_a (A^x_a)^2 = I$.
446	This gives us:
447	\begin{align}
448	\eqref{eq:anand-is-a-stable-genius-yet-again}
449	& = \E_\bx \sum_a \bra{\psi} (A^{\bx}_a)^2 \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} \ket{\psi} - \E_\bx \sum_a \bra{\psi} A^{\bx}_a \otimes B^{\bx}_a \ket{\psi}\nonumber\\
450	& = \E_\bx \sum_a \bra{\psi} (A^{\bx}_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}}) \cdot (A^{\bx}_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} - I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^{\bx}_a) \ket{\psi}\nonumber\\
451	& \leq \E_\bx \sum_a \Vert A^{\bx}_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} \ket{\psi}\Vert \cdot \Vert(A^{\bx}_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} - I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^{\bx}_a) \ket{\psi}\Vert\label{eq:anand-continues-to-be-a-stable-genius}
452	\end{align}
453	Now we apply Cauchy-Schwarz and then Jensen's inequality:
454	\begin{equation*}
455	\eqref{eq:anand-continues-to-be-a-stable-genius}
456	\leq  \sqrt{\E_\bx \sum_a \Vert A^{\bx}_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} \ket{\psi}\Vert^2
457		\cdot \sum_a \Vert(A^{\bx}_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} - I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^{\bx}_a) \ket{\psi}\Vert^2}
458	\end{equation*}
459	The first of these terms we bound by~$1$, and the second is~$O(\delta)$ by assumption.
460	\end{proof}
461	
462	\begin{remark}\label{remark:fraction-of-identity}
463	We note that the requirement in \Cref{fact:almost-agreement}
464	that one of the two measurements be projective is necessary.
465	Consider the measurements $\{A^x_a\}$ and $\{B^x_a\}$ with~$m$ separate outcomes~$a$
466	in which $A^x_a = B^x_a = I/m$ for all~$x$ and~$a$. Then $A^x_a \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}} \approx_{0} I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^x_a$, but as $m \rightarrow \infty$,
467	\begin{equation*}
468	1- \E_\bx \sum_a \bra{\psi} A^{\bx}_a \otimes B^{\bx}_a \ket{\psi} \rightarrow 1.
469	\end{equation*}
470	\end{remark}
471	
472	Thus, when one of the measurements is projective,
473	the ``$\approx_\delta$" distance is roughly equivalent to the ``$\consistency_\delta$" distance,
474	up to a polynomial factor (which we can tolerate losing in our proofs).
475	More generally, however, the ``$\approx_\delta$" distance
476	can be viewed as a weakening of the ``$\consistency_\delta$" distance.
477	In spite of this, we will spend much of the paper dealing with the ``$\approx_\delta$" distance,
478	as it is easier to manipulate but still strong enough to reach our desired consequences.
479	(See \cite[Section~$2.3.1$]{Vid11} for a further defense of this distance.)
480	We note that even when~$\ket{\psi}$ is a bipartite state,
481	the ``$\approx_\delta$" distance is defined for matrices~$Q$ and~$R$
482	which are not necessarily tensor products over the bipartition.
483	Such matrices will often be useful to pass through during intermediate steps of our proofs.
484	
485	A common use case for these distances is when the verifier (i) samples a pair of questions~$\bx = (\bx_0, \bx_1)$,
486	(ii) hands~$\bx_0$ to Alice and~$\bx_1$ second to Bob,
487	(iii) receives their answers $\ba_0$ and~$\ba_1$
488	and (iv) accepts if $f(\bx, \ba_0) = g(\bx, \ba_1)$ for some functions~$f$ and~$g$.
489	Write $\{A^{x_0}_{a_0}\}_{a_0}$ and $\{B^{x_1}_{a_1}\}_{a_1}$ for Alice and Bob's measurements, respectively.
490	We can view these as measurements which receive the pair~$(\bx_0, \bx_1)$ and simply ignore one coordinate.
491	Suppose the verifier accepts with probability $1-\delta$.
492	Using \Cref{not:marginalize}, we can view this as performing the consistency game
493	between the measurements $A^{x_0}_{[f(x, a_0) = b]}$ and  $B^{x_1}_{[f(x, a_1) = b]}$.
494	Hence, we can derive the following two facts:
495	\begin{equation*}
496	A^{x_0}_{[f(x, a_0) = b]} \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}}
497	\consistency_{\delta} I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^{x_1}_{[f(x, a_1) = b]},
498	\quad
499	\text{and}
500	\quad
501	A^{x_0}_{[f(x, a_0) = b]} \otimes I_{\reg{Bob}}
502	\approx_{\delta} I_{\reg{Alice}} \otimes B^{x_1}_{[f(x, a_1) = b]}.
503	\end{equation*}
504	This generic format will be the most common use of these notations.
505	
506	\Cref{remark:fraction-of-identity} highlights the importance of \emph{projective} measurements when dealing with the~``$\approx_\delta$" distance.
507	This, and several other key facts about the ``$\approx_\delta$" distance, are true only for projective measurements.
508	As a result, we will sometimes apply Naimark's theorem (\Cref{thm:naimark})
509	during our proofs to ``round" POVM measurements into projective measurements.
510	However, there is a subtlety in doing so, namely that 
511	because Naimark's theorem preserves measurement outcomes,
512	any ``$\consistency_\delta$" statements we have derived about our measurement operators will remain true,
513	but Naimark's theorem is \emph{not} guaranteed to preserve ``$\approx_\delta$" statements.
514	In this work, we will be able to dispense with this subtlety and assume all ``$\approx_\delta$" statements \emph{are} preserved,
515	because \emph{all ``$\approx_\delta$" statements in our proofs will be derived from ``$\consistency_\delta$" statements},
516	and so they will remain true after performing Naimark's theorem, since we could simply rederive them.
517	
518	\subsection{Miscellaneous properties of the state-dependent distances}
519	
520	In this section, we record some facts about the ``$\approx_\delta$" notation which we will use repeatedly throughout the paper.
521	A good rule of thumb is that everything one expects to be true about the ``$\approx_\delta$" notation actually \emph{is}  true,
522	except for those things which are not. 
523	As a result, we will be overly pedantic in this section in order to call attention to these cases.
524	
525	\subsubsection{Simple state-dependent distance facts}
